<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
    <AssemblyName>wsl-care-fake-tool</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>WslCare.FakeTool</RootNamespace>
    <IsPackable>false</IsPackable>
    <!--
      The stand-in for `docker`, `systemctl` and `journalctl` in the scenario harness: ONE program
      that answers as whichever tool its apphost was renamed to. The harness copies the apphost three
      times under the real tools' names into a folder it puts on PATH, alone, so the CLI under test can
      reach the fakes and nothing else.

      A C# program rather than shell scripts because the same fake must work on both CI legs, and on
      Windows a `docker.cmd` is NOT found when a program starts a bare `docker` without a shell
      (.NET's Process.Start and CreateProcess resolve `.exe` only) — so a script fake would be a
      second, different fake per OS. A renamed apphost is a real `docker.exe` / `docker` on each.
      It is also the product's language, per the family scenario rule.

      Reflection-free JSON, like the product: the fake reads and writes JSON with JsonDocument and
      Utf8JsonWriter only.
    -->
    <JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>false</JsonSerializerIsReflectionEnabledByDefault>
  </PropertyGroup>

</Project>
